1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,560
This is Doing and Feeling, Episode 4, Life as Computation. In 1948, at a symposium in Pasadena, the mathematician John von Neumann asked a question that sounds like an engineering puzzle and turns out to be a question about life.

2
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:19,040
Could a machine build a copy of itself?

3
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:21,220
The difficulty is a regress.

4
00:00:21,460 --> 00:00:24,740
To build a copy, the machine needs a description of itself.

5
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:27,220
But the description is part of the machine,

6
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:31,300
so it must contain a description of the description, and so on forever.

7
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:35,740
Von Neumann's way out was to use one description in two different ways.

8
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:39,800
A constructor reads the description and builds what it specifies.

9
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:44,060
A copier duplicates the description blindly, without interpreting it.

10
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:46,520
Build the offspring from the instructions,

11
00:00:46,860 --> 00:00:49,120
copy the instructions without reading them,

12
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:52,080
hand the copy over, and the regress disappears.

13
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:55,100
And if the copying is occasionally imperfect,

14
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:59,320
the offspring differ, which supplies the variation evolution needs.

15
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:03,500
This was five years before Watson and Crick described the double helix,

16
00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:06,420
yet it closely parallels how cells use DNA.

17
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:10,880
Genes are read, transcribed, and translated to build proteins,

18
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:16,760
and the same DNA is copied, base by base, without being interpreted, to make a new cell.

19
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:21,620
von Neumann had worked out the logical architecture of life from first principles.

20
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:27,340
This episode makes the strongest case I can for one claim and then tests it.

21
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:32,160
The claim is that life, intelligence, and computation are a single continuum.

22
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:33,820
It has four steps.

23
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:39,240
First, replicators arise naturally wherever computation and interaction meet.

24
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:46,300
leaps in complexity come from merging. Third, anything that keeps itself going must regulate,

25
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:52,200
and good regulation requires a model of what is being regulated, which makes prediction the core

26
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:58,560
of being alive. Fourth, the hardest things to predict are other predictors, so minds grew by

27
00:01:58,560 --> 00:02:04,780
modeling other minds. The most ambitious recent statement of this view is a 2025 book by Blaise

28
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:11,200
Aguera e. Arcus, published by MIT Press and the Antikythera Project and freely readable online.

29
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:18,140
Its title is a question, what is intelligence? I'll use his argument as a spine. The test comes

30
00:02:18,140 --> 00:02:23,220
at the end, and it is a serious one. If everything computes, does the claim say anything at all?

31
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:30,260
Start with replicators. Von Neumann went on to design a cell-for-producing pattern in a cellular

32
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:37,660
automaton, a grid of cells, each in one of 29 states, updated by local rules. It was published

33
00:02:37,660 --> 00:02:45,160
after his death in 1966. In 1970, John Conway's Game of Life showed that two states and a handful

34
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:52,340
of rules could support gliders, oscillators, and, eventually, universal computation. In 1984,

35
00:02:53,100 --> 00:02:57,940
Christopher Langton found a far simpler self-replicating pattern, now called Langton's

36
00:02:57,940 --> 00:03:05,660
loops. Then came artificial life in software. In 1991, the ecologist Thomas Ray released Tiara,

37
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:10,720
a virtual computer filled with self-copying programs competing for processor time.

38
00:03:11,220 --> 00:03:18,140
Parasites evolved that borrowed other programs' copying code and hosts evolved defenses. In 2003,

39
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:24,900
Richard Lensky, Charles Ofria, and colleagues used a platform called AVIDA to show how a complex

40
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:30,140
logical function could evolve through a series of simpler steps, some of them harmful when they

41
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:36,660
first arose. So the idea is old. But in Tiara, the first replicator was written by hand,

42
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:43,000
and Avidor rewards programs for doing useful logic. That's what makes a 2024 experiment by

43
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:48,580
Aguera E. Arcus and colleagues at Google, including Ben Lory and Alexander Mordvincev.

44
00:03:48,740 --> 00:03:54,420
So striking. They built a primordial soup of about 130,000 random programs,

45
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:56,440
each 64 bytes long,

46
00:03:56,700 --> 00:03:59,940
written in a variant of a famously minimal programming language

47
00:03:59,940 --> 00:04:01,580
whose name is unprintable.

48
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:03,440
They called the variant BFF.

49
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:06,100
Code and data lived on the same tape,

50
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:09,380
so programs could read and overwrite themselves and each other.

51
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:10,960
The procedure was simple.

52
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:12,980
Pick two programs at random,

53
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:14,000
glue them together,

54
00:04:14,340 --> 00:04:15,000
run the result,

55
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:16,720
split it back into two halves,

56
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:18,120
and return them to the soup.

57
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:19,900
Repeat millions of times.

58
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:21,660
There was no fitness function,

59
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:22,660
no reward,

60
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:24,240
and no handwritten ancestor.

61
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:26,840
For a long time, nothing much happened.

62
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:30,980
Then, in about 40% of runs, there was a sudden transition.

63
00:04:31,440 --> 00:04:35,460
Self-replicating programs appeared, spread, and took over the soup,

64
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:38,180
and the measured complexity of the population jumped.

65
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:41,460
It happened even with random mutations switched off,

66
00:04:41,580 --> 00:04:45,480
so the replicators were assembled through interaction and self-modification

67
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:48,540
rather than found by luck in the initial noise.

68
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,440
Surely the instruction set is doing a lot of the work.

69
00:04:52,440 --> 00:04:55,080
The authors tested exactly that.

70
00:04:55,540 --> 00:04:59,240
Replicators also emerged in versions built on the fourth language

71
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:03,100
and on the instruction set of the old Z80 microprocessor.

72
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:07,520
In a minimal language called Sublec, they never arose on their own,

73
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:10,300
though replicators planted by hand could spread.

74
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:12,080
So the substrate matters.

75
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:15,940
What the experiment shows is that under fairly generic conditions,

76
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:20,960
replication can arise without design, and once it arises, dominates.

77
00:05:22,440 --> 00:05:29,440
is how chemistry did it, or that the soup goes on evolving open-ended novelty. Aguera E. Arcus's

78
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:35,900
reading is that life is an attractor that computation falls into. The second step is merging.

79
00:05:36,260 --> 00:05:43,900
In the classic picture, complexity grows by small mutations filtered by competition. Aguera E. Arcus

80
00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:49,220
argues that the biggest leaps came from symbiogenesis, the fusion of once-independent

81
00:05:49,220 --> 00:05:56,660
organisms. Around 1905 to 1910, the Russian botanist Konstantin Marischkowski proposed that

82
00:05:56,660 --> 00:06:02,480
chloroplasts, the photosynthetic machinery in plant cells, were once free-living bacteria.

83
00:06:02,820 --> 00:06:09,440
The idea was largely ignored until Lin Margiulis revived and extended it in a 1967 paper,

84
00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:15,240
published under the name Lin Sagan and reportedly rejected by about 15 journals first.

85
00:06:15,880 --> 00:06:17,600
Today it's textbook biology.

86
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:23,400
The mitochondria in your cells descend from bacteria engulfed by an ancestral cell roughly

87
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:27,320
2 billion years ago, and they still carry small genomes of their own.

88
00:06:27,480 --> 00:06:34,560
In 1995, John Maynard Smith and Irsh Satmari generalized the pattern in the major transitions

89
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:40,600
in evolution, replicating molecules joining into chromosomes, simple cells merging into

90
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:46,860
complex ones, single cells forming multicellular bodies, solitary animals forming colonies,

91
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:53,300
and human societies held together by language. In each transition, entities that once reproduced

92
00:06:53,300 --> 00:07:00,340
independently come to reproduce only as parts of a larger whole. Aguera E. Arcus reads the BFF

93
00:07:00,340 --> 00:07:05,700
soup the same way, with replicators assembled from cooperating fragments, and he argues that

94
00:07:05,700 --> 00:07:11,540
humans and machine intelligence are entering such a transition now. That last claim is a prediction,

95
00:07:11,540 --> 00:07:17,840
and we'll meet a rival reading of it shortly. The third step is the one that turns life into

96
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:24,660
prediction, and it runs through physics. In 1943, Erwin Schrödinger lectured in Dublin on a question

97
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:29,420
he published as What is Life? How do living things stay ordered against the second law of

98
00:07:29,420 --> 00:07:36,080
thermodynamics. They feed, he said, on negative entropy, maintaining internal order by exporting

99
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:41,440
disorder to their surroundings. He also guessed that heredity must be stored in an aperiodic

100
00:07:41,440 --> 00:07:48,260
crystal, orderly but not repetitive, an idea that inspired both Crick and Watson. Here's the bridge

101
00:07:48,260 --> 00:07:54,980
I find most compelling. In 2012, Suzanne Still and colleagues proved a theorem about a broad

102
00:07:54,980 --> 00:08:01,060
class of systems driven by a fluctuating environment. Such a system inevitably retains

103
00:08:01,060 --> 00:08:06,520
information about the past. Some of that information helps predict the future. The rest,

104
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:11,740
which they called nostalgia, is useless for prediction, and the energy the system wastes

105
00:08:11,740 --> 00:08:17,400
as heat is proportional to how much of it the system keeps. In other words, a memory that does

106
00:08:17,400 --> 00:08:23,120
not predict is thermodynamically wasteful. Efficiency and prediction are the same pressure,

107
00:08:25,520 --> 00:08:33,880
Which ties back to episode 3. If good prediction is good compression, then life is under physical pressure to compress its world.

108
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:39,020
And that's exactly where one recent attempt to define life has run into trouble.

109
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:46,000
In a 2023 paper in Nature, Lee Cronin, Sarah Walker, and colleagues argued for assembly theory,

110
00:08:46,180 --> 00:08:50,460
which scores an object by the minimum number of joining steps needed to build it,

111
00:08:50,600 --> 00:08:55,740
and treats complex objects found in many copies as a signature of selection and life.

112
00:08:55,980 --> 00:09:01,200
Critics led by Hector Zaniel replied that the measure is essentially a weak form of compression,

113
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:07,320
a cousin of the algorithms behind zip files, and that it neither explains nor quantifies selection.

114
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:13,460
The assembly theorists reject that comparison, and the argument continues. Either way,

115
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:18,960
it shows how quickly attempts to define life end up in the information theory of episode 3.

116
00:09:19,180 --> 00:09:26,100
One more definition matters for later. In the early 1970s, the Chilean biologists Humberto

117
00:09:26,100 --> 00:09:33,440
Maturana and Francisco Varela defined a living system as autopoietic, self-producing, a network

118
00:09:33,440 --> 00:09:38,480
of processes that continually makes the very components that make it up. Notice the difference

119
00:09:38,480 --> 00:09:45,640
from von Neumann. His replicator is defined by a description at Coppies. Autopoiesis is defined by

120
00:09:45,640 --> 00:09:51,020
a body that keeps rebuilding itself. That difference will become the center of the skeptic's case in

121
00:09:51,020 --> 00:09:59,940
episode 13. Now regulation. In 1943, Archer O. Rosenbluth, Norbert Wiener, and Julian Bidillo

122
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:06,420
published a short paper called Behavior, Purpose, and Teleology. Purposeful behavior, they proposed,

123
00:10:06,660 --> 00:10:11,600
is simply behavior controlled by negative feedback. A thermostat that turns on the heat

124
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:17,540
when the room gets cold is, in this minimal sense, pursuing a goal. Wiener named the field

125
00:10:17,540 --> 00:10:25,620
cybernetics in 1948. W. Ross Ashby added the law of requisite variety. A regulator must be able to

126
00:10:25,620 --> 00:10:32,140
produce at least as much variety as the disturbances it is trying to cancel. In 1970, Roger

127
00:10:32,140 --> 00:10:37,340
Canant and Ashby published a theorem with a wonderful title, every good regulator of a system

128
00:10:37,340 --> 00:10:42,680
must be a model of that system. The careful statement is narrower than the slogan. They

129
00:10:42,680 --> 00:10:50,120
He showed that the simplest regulator achieving optimal results must map the system's states onto its own, in effect mirroring it.

130
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:56,060
The slogan, that every controller secretly contains a world model, goes further than the proof.

131
00:10:56,300 --> 00:11:00,460
That's one justification for model-based reinforcement learning.

132
00:11:00,680 --> 00:11:05,600
If you want to control an environment well, learn a model of how it responds.

133
00:11:05,940 --> 00:11:12,400
And in 2025, Jonathan Richens and colleagues gave the idea a modern, precise form.

134
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:17,800
They proved that any agent able to generalize across a wide enough range of multi-step,

135
00:11:18,060 --> 00:11:22,200
goal-directed tasks must have learned a predictive model of its environment,

136
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:25,540
one that can in principle be extracted from its behavior,

137
00:11:25,780 --> 00:11:29,280
and that more capable agents require more accurate models.

138
00:11:29,620 --> 00:11:32,940
That's the bridge Aguera e. Arcus's argument needs.

139
00:11:33,460 --> 00:11:37,980
Survival requires regulation, flexible regulation requires a model,

140
00:11:38,180 --> 00:11:41,320
and a model that runs ahead of the present is a predictor.

141
00:11:42,680 --> 00:11:45,360
The fourth step, intelligence becomes social.

142
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:49,120
Much of any animal's environment consists of other animals,

143
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:52,780
and the hardest things in the world to predict are other predictors.

144
00:11:52,980 --> 00:11:59,040
In 1976, the psychologist Nicholas Humphrey published The Social Function of Intellect.

145
00:11:59,360 --> 00:12:01,720
His argument was that for many primates,

146
00:12:02,040 --> 00:12:06,740
the practical problems of daily life are not that demanding, but social life is.

147
00:12:07,240 --> 00:12:11,520
Alliances, deception, reciprocity, remembering who did what to whom.

148
00:12:12,680 --> 00:12:16,660
he proposed, evolved mainly to handle other primates.

149
00:12:17,060 --> 00:12:22,220
Richard Byrne and Andrew Whiten developed this into the Machiavellian intelligence hypothesis,

150
00:12:22,720 --> 00:12:25,780
and Robin Dunbar found that across primate species,

151
00:12:26,120 --> 00:12:30,580
the relative size of the neocortex tracks typical social group size.

152
00:12:30,900 --> 00:12:37,360
Extrapolated to humans, it suggests a natural group of about 150, now called Dunbar's number.

153
00:12:37,740 --> 00:12:40,060
The correlation has been challenged, too.

154
00:12:40,560 --> 00:12:44,540
A 2017 study of more than 140 primate species

155
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:48,740
found that diet predicted brain size better than group size did.

156
00:12:49,380 --> 00:12:51,760
Aguera e. Arcus turns this into an engine.

157
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,240
If I predict you and you predict me,

158
00:12:54,540 --> 00:12:57,040
each of us gains by modeling the other's model,

159
00:12:57,320 --> 00:12:58,960
including the other's model of us.

160
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:03,340
That recursive mutual modeling is an arms race with no natural ceiling.

161
00:13:03,660 --> 00:13:06,720
And once a brain has machinery for modeling other minds,

162
00:13:06,920 --> 00:13:08,900
it can turn that machinery on itself.

163
00:13:10,120 --> 00:13:13,420
On this view, is theory of mind pointed inward.

164
00:13:13,740 --> 00:13:16,160
The idea has a surprising ancestor.

165
00:13:16,500 --> 00:13:22,740
In a section added to the Gay Science in 1887, Friedrich Nietzsche argued that consciousness

166
00:13:22,740 --> 00:13:27,560
developed only under the pressure of the need to communicate, which is why, he said,

167
00:13:27,880 --> 00:13:30,240
it is the most superficial part of the mind.

168
00:13:30,580 --> 00:13:35,460
You do not need his contempt to see the outline of modern theories, including the attention

169
00:13:35,460 --> 00:13:38,280
schema theory we'll examine in episode 8.

170
00:13:40,060 --> 00:13:45,880
E. Arcus carries the continuum all the way to consciousness and free will, through a relational,

171
00:13:46,260 --> 00:13:51,700
social account, though his confidence is greatest about intelligence. We'll weigh his view of

172
00:13:51,700 --> 00:13:58,420
consciousness in episode 8, and the fourth test below targets that last step. Now the test,

173
00:13:58,600 --> 00:14:03,820
because a theory this sweeping deserves pressure. The first objection is pancomputationalism.

174
00:14:04,200 --> 00:14:09,000
In 1988, Hillary Putnam argued that, under a suitably contrived mapping,

175
00:14:09,440 --> 00:14:13,300
any ordinary physical system implements any finite-state automaton you like.

176
00:14:13,740 --> 00:14:15,460
John Searle put it more memorably.

177
00:14:15,680 --> 00:14:20,560
The wall behind him, he said, could be described as running the word-star word processor.

178
00:14:20,840 --> 00:14:28,100
If computation is that cheap, then life is computation and minds are computation become trivially true and say nothing.

179
00:14:28,420 --> 00:14:30,460
That always struck me as a trick.

180
00:14:30,460 --> 00:14:35,460
The mapping is invented after the fact, state by state, and it predicts nothing.

181
00:14:35,840 --> 00:14:38,580
That's essentially David Chalmers' reply.

182
00:14:38,840 --> 00:14:45,180
In 1996, he published a paper asking whether a rock implements every finite-state automaton.

183
00:14:45,780 --> 00:14:50,720
Real implementation, he argued, requires the right counterfactual structure.

184
00:14:50,940 --> 00:14:54,960
The physical system must be disposed to follow the computation's transitions,

185
00:14:55,320 --> 00:15:00,340
including in situations that never actually occur, and it must respond to inputs.

186
00:15:00,460 --> 00:15:03,700
A wall has no such dispositions for word star.

187
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:09,100
On that stricter notion, saying a cell computes is a substantive and testable claim,

188
00:15:09,280 --> 00:15:11,840
and a cell copying its DNA passes it.

189
00:15:12,180 --> 00:15:13,360
The wall does not.

190
00:15:13,600 --> 00:15:15,880
The second objection is about purpose.

191
00:15:16,200 --> 00:15:21,560
The cybernetic slogan that the purpose of a system is what it does makes every effect a purpose,

192
00:15:21,940 --> 00:15:24,900
so the purpose of the heart would include making thumping noises.

193
00:15:25,800 --> 00:15:30,120
Philosophers of biology like Ruth Milliken distinguish an effect from a function,

194
00:15:30,460 --> 00:15:33,080
an effect that explains why the trait was selected.

195
00:15:33,440 --> 00:15:36,440
The evolutionary story can supply that distinction.

196
00:15:36,880 --> 00:15:38,500
The slogan alone cannot.

197
00:15:38,900 --> 00:15:41,080
The third objection targets the claim

198
00:15:41,080 --> 00:15:43,640
that language models mark a new major transition

199
00:15:43,640 --> 00:15:44,400
in intelligence.

200
00:15:44,760 --> 00:15:48,660
In 2025, Henry Farrell, Alison Gopnik,

201
00:15:49,240 --> 00:15:52,420
Cosma Shalizzi, and James Evans argued in science

202
00:15:52,420 --> 00:15:54,480
that large models are better understood

203
00:15:54,480 --> 00:15:56,420
as cultural and social technologies,

204
00:15:56,820 --> 00:16:00,180
like writing, print, markets, and bureaucracies.

205
00:16:00,460 --> 00:16:10,560
On their view, these models are powerful new ways for humans to store, access, and recombine what other humans have produced, rather than new agents in their own right.

206
00:16:10,880 --> 00:16:17,160
That reading can grant that these systems are superb predictors, while denying that a new kind of mind has appeared.

207
00:16:17,420 --> 00:16:23,860
And the fourth objection is the one that matters most for this series, the slide from intelligence to consciousness.

208
00:16:24,400 --> 00:16:27,840
The first steps of the argument that life involves computation,

209
00:16:28,280 --> 00:16:30,500
that intelligence is largely prediction,

210
00:16:30,780 --> 00:16:33,260
and that minds were shaped by social modeling,

211
00:16:33,580 --> 00:16:37,020
all have serious defenders, though each is contested.

212
00:16:37,220 --> 00:16:42,500
The final step, that a system with the right predictive and social modeling thereby has experience,

213
00:16:42,860 --> 00:16:47,700
is exactly what biological naturalists and integrated information theory deny,

214
00:16:48,020 --> 00:16:50,460
as we'll see in episodes 9 and 13.

215
00:16:51,340 --> 00:16:54,860
Aguera e. Arcus's relational view is a coherent answer,

216
00:16:55,060 --> 00:17:00,020
but it's one that a dualist, a pansychist, or anil seth would reject at the root.

217
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:04,200
So how much of the continuum survives the tests?

218
00:17:04,580 --> 00:17:06,540
Most of the first three steps.

219
00:17:06,740 --> 00:17:09,900
The computational view of life is more than a metaphor.

220
00:17:10,440 --> 00:17:14,120
Von Neumann's architecture closely parallels how cells reproduce,

221
00:17:14,120 --> 00:17:18,540
and the BFF soup shows that replication can emerge from computation

222
00:17:20,760 --> 00:17:26,200
design. Regulation requires modeling, and physics penalizes memories that do not predict,

223
00:17:26,440 --> 00:17:31,720
so prediction sits at the heart of anything that keeps itself going. And the hardest prediction

